Yes, that is the plan, and this PR is deliberately the easy part of it.

These four are the only components left that own no `framework.*` configuration key, so they need nothing beyond moving the service file and the compiler pass registration. Everything else owns a key, and moving one means `framework.<key>` has to keep working. #65884 adds the mechanism for that, and #65885 is the first user of it.

From surveying the rest, they fall into three groups.

Ready once #65884 lands, with no other work: Semaphore, WebLink and RemoteEvent. Nothing else in `FrameworkExtension` reads their state.

Ready, but each needs one coupling inverted into a compiler pass, because another section currently removes a definition based on `readConfigEnabled()`: HtmlSanitizer (the form extension), TypeInfo (the json_streamer error), PropertyAccess (two serializer services), Lock (five reads in RateLimiter and Messenger, all answerable with `$container->hasAlias('lock.factory')`, which is more accurate than today), and Uid (`argument_resolver.uid` moves into the bundle).

Not yet: Scheduler, because `scheduler.php` needs `messenger.receiver_locator` and `AddScheduleMessengerPass` needs `messenger.transport_factory` and `messenger.default_serializer`, all of which only FrameworkBundle defines. A SchedulerBundle that cannot boot without FrameworkBundle would defeat the purpose, so it waits for Messenger.

Messenger and Cache are the interesting ones you named, and they are the hardest. Cache is the substrate: `cache.validator`, `cache.serializer`, `cache.property_access` and friends are all created by `registerCacheConfiguration()`, so several other moves depend on how that is handled. The bigger obstacle for both, and for Serializer, Validator and Translation, is that their `*_debug.php` decorates a service with a `Traceable*` class. A data collector is easy, since `ProfilerPass` already does nothing when there is no `profiler` service, so the tag can simply always be there. A decorator is unconditional once declared, so those bundles need to know at load time whether the profiler is on, which is exactly what the isolated container in `MergeExtensionConfigurationPass` prevents. That needs its own answer before Cache or Messenger can move.

So: yes for most of them, in stacked PRs, and no for Scheduler, Cache and Messenger until those two problems are solved.

