by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-07T20:01:13Z

Correcting myself on the previous comment: I claimed the `Traceable*` decorators were a blocker for Cache and Messenger, and that is wrong.

I checked every `*_debug.php` in FrameworkBundle. The only reference any of them makes to a profiler-owned service is `profiler.is_disabled_state_checker` in `validator_debug.php`, and it is already `nullOnInvalid()`. Everything else each file references is either its own component's service or shared infrastructure such as `event_dispatcher` and `debug.file_link_formatter`. In particular the data collector is declared in the same file as the decorator that consumes it, so there is no missing service to work around.

I verified by compiling a container with the `serializer_debug.php` shape and no `profiler` service at all: it compiles, and `serializer` still resolves to `TraceableSerializer`. `ProfilerPass` simply does nothing when there is no profiler, so the collector is never attached to anything.

So a component bundle does not need load-time knowledge of whether the profiler is enabled. It gates on `kernel.debug`, which is a Kernel parameter every bundle can read, and uses ignore-on-invalid for anything profiler-owned. `WorkflowBundle` in #65885 already does exactly this: it imports `workflow_debug.php` under `kernel.debug`, and `WorkflowDebugPass` decorates each workflow with `TraceableWorkflow` while referencing `profiler.is_disabled_state_checker` with `IGNORE_ON_INVALID_REFERENCE`.

The one real behavioral difference is narrow. FrameworkBundle loads these files when the profiler is enabled, whereas a component bundle would load them whenever `kernel.debug` is true. Those differ only when debug is on and the profiler is explicitly disabled, where you get a traceable wrapper that nothing reads. That is dev-only overhead, not a breakage, and a compiler pass can drop the decorator when `profiler` is absent if it is worth avoiding.

Cache and Messenger are still the hardest of the set, but for their own reasons rather than this one. Cache is the substrate that `cache.validator`, `cache.serializer` and `cache.property_access` all come from, so several other moves depend on how it is handled, and Messenger is what Scheduler is waiting on.
